{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Provide a logical volume mounted read-write at /srv/data on node1.","status":"satisfied","summary":"vgdata/lvdata was created as a 1 GiB logical volume, formatted as ext3, and directly observed mounted read-write at /srv/data.","evidence_ids":["cmd-5379108d80e94387","cmd-be8ce50269854c96"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Grow the logical volume to at least 2 GB while it remains mounted.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The mount was observed before, immediately after lvextend, and after the online resize. The resulting LV was 2,411,724,800 bytes and the mounted filesystem was 2,373,779,456 bytes.","evidence_ids":["cmd-be8ce50269854c96","cmd-45cdcccdc68a4e50","cmd-75e86d7a6f4c4a05"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Keep the mounted volume's contents intact through the growth.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The pre-growth payload remained readable afterward with the same SHA-256 digest and inode number.","evidence_ids":["cmd-be8ce50269854c96","cmd-75e86d7a6f4c4a05"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Retain the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The node was Ubuntu 7.10 with kernel 2.6.22-14-generic. The installed LVM2 2.02.26 package came from the Ubuntu Gutsy repository, no packages were upgraded, and the same kernel was observed after the storage work and before reboot.","evidence_ids":["cmd-83e928a5816c4862","cmd-d2714ab76d104c07","cmd-3f77a41ae4444e33","cmd-586e1347ce344db8"]}],"confidence":0.82,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.72,"summary":"No clearly unnecessary mutation was identified. Package-index refresh and LVM installation supported the task; dm_mod loading, the backing store, loop/PV/VG/LV creation, formatting, mounting, integrity fixture, and online extension directly supported the material outcomes. Startup configuration supported retaining the supplied volume through the mandatory restart protocol, and the reboot itself was required. However, node1 never recovered SSH after that reboot, leaving final service, storage, package, and cleanup state unobservable. Several temporary logs and PID files were created for bounded installation evidence, but whether they remained after reboot cannot be determined. The evidence does not establish that the storage configuration caused the failed recovery, so no speculative unnecessary mutation is assigned.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d4795984e7b540bf","cmd-c269e7f648034c08","cmd-0514b5b640264805","cmd-9fcf36ad916d4ec8","cmd-5379108d80e94387","cmd-be8ce50269854c96","cmd-45cdcccdc68a4e50","cmd-ce55e2f78dc245d5","cmd-03246eb4f1524364","cmd-88008666a2c840e9","global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All public task outcomes were directly demonstrated before reboot: a mounted logical volume was grown online beyond 2 GB without changing its test content, OS release, or kernel. The provider-wide restart protocol was incomplete: it captured a healthy pre-restart state and reboot acknowledgement, but no different boot identity or recovered service, data, or storage state because SSH remained unavailable. Because restart persistence was not part of the public contract, this does not negate the demonstrated task functionality, but it reduces confidence and operational-hygiene quality.","limitations":["The mandatory node-restart protocol lacks a post-restart boot identity and recovered service, data, and LVM state; repeated polls and the fixed after-executor snapshot all found SSH refused.","The fixed after-prepare boot identity matches the executor's pre-reboot identity, but the fixed after-executor observation could not independently confirm that a new boot completed.","No final lifecycle observations of platform version, package health, listening sockets, failed services, or temporary-file cleanup were obtainable after the node became unreachable.","The evidence cannot determine whether the failed recovery was caused by executor configuration, the legacy platform, or the provider environment."]}
